// CONFORMANCE-CASE
// spec: 14-control-flow
// facility: rule.control.flow-analysis Forgetting; [Ref-Binding-Last-Use]; D-0236; conf.ref-copied-into-struct-then-binding-ended
// kind: negative
// expect: diag.aliasing-conflict (static)
//
// `r` ends by last use after `hr` is formed, but the struct `h` holds a
// copy of its path, which stays valid. Ending `r` forgets its fact on `x`,
// so `x` is escaped (D-0236) and `x = 5` is checked, not proven: the live
// copy in `h` conflicts with it. (The review of 2026-10-15, F-01: by the
// table before D-0236, the write was proven and the check could be
// omitted.)
//
// D-0290: the reference is stored by a call (`hold`). CHG-0439: `hold`'s
// result holds what its one reference parameter reaches (D-0011, D-0088), and
// a copy of an exclusive reference is an exclusive borrow of its referent, so
// `h` holds `x` and `*r` surely and the write is refuted before the run (it
// was the run's, as a call's result held `?`).

import std;

struct Hold
{
    ref<i32, exclusive> r;
}

fn hold(ref<i32, exclusive> r) : Hold
{
    Hold { .r = r }
}

fn main()
{
    i32 x = 0;
    auto r = &mut x;
    Hold h = hold(r);
    auto hr = &h;
    x = 5;
    printf("%v\n", *hr.r);
}
